 \section{Post-hackathon Outcomes}
 \label{sec:outcomes}
 
-A \href{https://github.com/AC-BO-Hackathon/ac-bo-hackathon.github.io/issues/172\#issuecomment-5348017006}{follow-up review of public records} provides a snapshot of longer-term outcomes. Of the 126 paper co-authors, 74 (59\%) were students at the time of the hackathon; by August 2026, 96 (76\%) had a publicly visible post-event output in or adjacent to BO, active learning, or self-driving laboratories, including 57 of the 74 students (77\%). Roughly 19 of the 45 projects showed a continuation trail through a manuscript, software tool, thesis chapter, or maintained benchmark.
+A follow-up review of public records (publications, code repositories, and professional profiles), compiled in August 2026 with an evidence link recorded for each person, provides a snapshot of longer-term outcomes~\cite{acbo_followup_2026}. Of the 124 paper co-authors, 73 (59\%) were students at the time of the hackathon; by August 2026, 94 (76\%) had a publicly visible post-event output in or adjacent to BO, active learning, or self-driving laboratories, including 56 of the 73 students (77\%). Roughly 19 of the 45 projects showed a continuation trail through a manuscript, software tool, thesis chapter, or maintained benchmark.
 
-Several of these continuations can be traced to specific manuscripts. Project 44's work on rank-based BO became \href{https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0272663}{\emph{Ranking over regression for Bayesian optimization and molecule selection}} (\emph{APL Machine Learning}, 2025), which explicitly acknowledges the hackathon. Project 45's generality-oriented BO work continued as \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18966}{\emph{One Set to Rule Them All: How to Obtain General Chemical Conditions via Bayesian Optimization over Curried Functions}} (CurryBO) and \href{https://doi.org/10.1039/D5DD00039D}{\emph{BoTier: multi-objective Bayesian optimization with tiered objective structures}} (\emph{Digital Discovery}, 2025). Project 15 was followed by \href{https://doi.org/10.1039/D4DD00225C}{\emph{Cost-informed Bayesian reaction optimization}} (\emph{Digital Discovery}, 2024), while projects 30 and 31 developed into the SeroOpt software and \href{https://doi.org/10.1039/D5DD00005J}{\emph{Machine-learning-guided design of electroanalytical pulse waveforms}} (\emph{Digital Discovery}, 2025). Project 36 continued as the LookaHES manuscript, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06505}{\emph{Neural nonmyopic Bayesian optimization in dynamic cost settings}}, and the ScattBO benchmark from project 24 informed the later autonomous-synthesis study \href{https://doi.org/10.1021/acsnano.5c15488}{\emph{Autonomous synthesis of nanoparticles with target scattering patterns}} (\emph{ACS Nano}, 2026).
